\section{Classes Describe Objects; Generic Functions Describe Operations}

The 1988 \textit{Common Lisp Object System Specification} by Daniel
G. Bobrow and colleagues separates instance structure from the
definition of an operation. A class describes its instances, while
a generic function supplies an interface implemented by methods.
Methods may specialize several required parameters, using classes
or, in an \texttt{eql} specializer, a particular value. For example,
the appropriate method for transferring a record can depend on
both its source and destination, without artificially assigning
the operation to either object. David B. Lamkins's \textit{Successful
Lisp} contrasts this dispatch on argument specializers with deciding
everything inside one body by explicitly testing argument types.

At a call, CLOS orders applicable methods by specificity, using
the class precedence lists and the generic function's argument
precedence order (left to right by default). Method combination
determines how that ordered set executes. With standard method
combination, the most specific applicable \texttt{:around} method,
if present, can choose whether to call the next method. At the core,
\texttt{:before} methods run from most to least specific; then the
most specific primary method runs and may explicitly invoke less
specific primaries with \texttt{call-next-method}; on normal return,
\texttt{:after} methods run in the opposite order. An \texttt{:after}
method is therefore not an unconditional cleanup action: use
\texttt{unwind-protect} for that purpose. A dispatch cache may accelerate
these decisions, but method or class changes must not leave a stale
effective method observable.

For a concrete dispatch test, consider three primary methods for
\texttt{transfer}, specialized on \texttt{(account account)},
\texttt{(savings-account account)}, and
\texttt{(account managed-account)}, where the latter two classes
each inherit directly from \texttt{account}. Pass a savings account
as source and a managed account as target. Under the default
argument order, the second method wins because its first argument
specializer is more specific. With a target-first precedence order,
the third wins. This is neither an ambiguity resolved by definition
order nor a rule that adds up inheritance distances. Test both
calls with the same objects to isolate argument precedence from
class membership.

\section{Detection, Policy, and Recovery}

Peter Seibel's \textit{Practical Common Lisp} presents conditions
and restarts as separate roles: one party knows what happened,
another knows how the operation can recover, and another can choose
a policy. A condition is an object representing a situation, not a
command to unwind the stack. In particular, a handler installed
with \texttt{handler-bind} runs before unwinding to any handler's
establishment point; returning normally from such a handler does
not itself handle an error. Micha{\l} Herda's \textit{The Common Lisp
Condition System} distinguishes a general restart binding from
\texttt{restart-case}, whose clauses are invoked by transferring
control to the corresponding recovery point. A debugger can offer
the same dynamically established recovery choices that programmatic
handlers use.

\section{Worked Example: Recover Without Retrying the Caller}

\begin{verbatim}
(define-condition missing-reading (error) ())

(defun reading-or-restart (x)
  (if (numberp x)
      x
      (restart-case (error 'missing-reading)
        (use-reading (value) value))))

(handler-bind
    ((missing-reading
       (lambda (c)
         (let ((r (find-restart 'use-reading c)))
           (when r (invoke-restart r 12))))))
  (+ 1 (reading-or-restart nil))) ; => 13
\end{verbatim}

The callee signals \texttt{missing-reading} while its restart is
available. The handler looks for a restart applicable in the context
of that condition and invokes it explicitly. The transfer abandons
the failing path and makes the \texttt{restart-case} return \(12\).
It does not abandon the surrounding addition. With a numeric input
the handler must never run; with a nonnumeric input and no selecting
handler, an unhandled \texttt{error} normally enters the debugger
rather than quietly returning a default. These three cases check
distinct parts of the control protocol.

In \Cref{fig:visual-155}, the restart rejoins the path that returns to
the addition; it does not return to the signaling expression.

