If two nested library calls offer similarly named restarts, the
condition context and the exact restart object matter; the name
alone is not a durable reference to a recovery operation. A client
also should not assume that selecting a restart replays everything
that led to the failure. The restart's own contract determines
whether it retries, supplies a substitute, or exits altogether.
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-156} discusses more general access to evaluation
state; the condition protocol is useful precisely because it exposes
specific recovery choices without exposing arbitrary stack mutation.
