\section{Tracing Standard Method Combination Under a Nonlocal Exit}

A generic function test can record not only the final value
but also the methods that reached their bodies. Model one
call under standard CLOS method combination, with all applicable
methods already sorted by decreasing specificity within their
qualifier. Assume a primary exists and calls to the next method
keep the same arguments. The inputs are the four ordered lists
and the argument values. The output is the returned values,
or a nonlocal transfer, together with the trace emitted by the
method bodies. Auxiliary methods may append to the trace; a
driver outside the generic function handles any nonlocal transfer
and examines those events. This isolates combination from the
separate question of how the method order was calculated.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input sorted lists $A,B,P,F$, argument tuple $x$
  \Output effective call, preserving nonlocal transfers
  \Function{Core}{}
    \ForAll{$b$ in $B$}
      \State \Call{CallMethod}{$b,x$}
    \EndFor
    \State $v\gets\Call{PrimaryChain}{P,x}$
    \Comment{The primary must opt in to its successor}
    \ForAll{$f$ in reverse order of $F$}
      \State \Call{CallMethod}{$f,x$}
      \Comment{Reached only on normal return}
    \EndFor
    \State \Return all values in $v$
  \EndFunction
  \State \Return $\Call{AroundChain}{A,x,\mathit{core}}$
\end{sudocode}

Let a call on a \texttt{premium} account select \texttt{around-P},
two before methods \texttt{before-P} and \texttt{before-A}, one
primary \texttt{primary-P}, and two after methods \texttt{after-P}
and \texttt{after-A}. The around method logs \texttt{enter}, calls
its successor, then logs \texttt{leave}. On normal input the
trace is \texttt{enter}, both befores ($P,A$), the primary,
both afters ($A,P$), and \texttt{leave}. If the primary
instead logs its name and throws the value $4$ to an outer
catch, that catch receives $4$. The trace ends at the primary;
neither after method nor the trailing around code executes.
An \texttt{unwind-protect} cleanup could record a separate
event even in this second run.

The core is entered only if the around chain delegates to it.
If the around method returns a cached value directly, no
before or after methods execute. On normal completion the
primary's values, not the after methods' values, pass through
the core. A test should include such a short circuit and
distinct auxiliary events: looking only at the final value
would not detect a reversed after order.
